views {

  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // Four walkthroughs and nothing else. Every node on a page is either pointed at
  // by a step or sits inside a box a step points at.
  //   1 · the plan     — a person turns an idea into nine tickets
  //   2 · the run      — the installer puts `sf` on your path, then ticket after ticket
  //   3 · the log      — what a run writes and how to read a stuck tower
  //   4 · the failures — the same run with every alarm firing
  // A step here says only what happens at that moment. What a thing *is*, and
  // every rule and table behind it, lives in that node's own description in
  // model.c4 — click the node, not the step. Rules for editing these files:
  // ./REFERENCE.md — the code is the only source of truth.
  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  // ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // 1 · THE PLANNING PHASE
  // ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  dynamic view index {
    title "Spider Factory/Walkthrough/1 · The planning phase — a vague idea becomes nine tickets"

    include human, planAgents, planAgents.*, beads
    include tower, tower.epic, tower.epic.epicE2e, tower.epic.epicReview
    include tower.featA, tower.featA.backendA, tower.featA.unitA, tower.featA.frontendA
    include tower.featA.featureTestA, tower.featA.featureReviewA

    // ═ ACT I — the planning session: three conversations, and every row they write

    human -> planAgents.planEpic "a vague idea: I want somewhere to keep my records" {
      title "You describe the idea; the planner asks questions and settles it"
      notes """
It surveys what the repo already has and reads it, then asks — one or two questions at a time, never a
questionnaire — until the idea is settled. It agrees exactly one epic and one name for it,
`epic/record-box`, and then writes the three rows below, in that order: the gate, the journeys, the review.
There is no merge ticket at this tier or any other. Nothing runs yet.
"""
    }

    planAgents.planEpic -[plans]-> tower.epic "the epic gate — the gate, written first" {
      title "The one row that carries the epic's branch name"
      notes """
The gate goes in before any ticket, because it is the only row anywhere that carries a branch name, and the
two rows below reach it through their own ids, which copy its uuid. It carries one key,
`branch: epic/record-box`, and nothing
else: not where its pull request goes (`main`, derived — it is its own top of tower) and not which tier it is
(the kind word of its own id says `epic`, and nothing is read from the database to work that out).
"""
    }

    planAgents.planEpic -[plans]-> tower.epic.epicE2e "the end-to-end ticket — journeys in a real browser" {
      title "The broad tier of the test system"
    }

    planAgents.planEpic -[plans]-> tower.epic.epicReview "the epic review ticket — the three questions" {
      title "The epic's review — what you wait for before the tier is proved"
      notes """
Both these rows block the gate, and that set of blockers is the whole epic tier — the gate's own panel lists
them. Nothing is assembled for the journeys: the lead agent merges each feature into the epic branch as its
own gate closes.
"""
    }

    human -> planAgents.planFeature "break the epic into features" {
      title "One epic in, its features out — this epic needs only one"
      notes """
It runs off an epic id; given none it asks one question, and "there is no epic" is a legal answer — though a
feature with no epic gate above it is not work: the loop reads the epic out of its id, finds no such gate,
refuses it and logs the ticket id, so the planner says so in its report rather than shipping a tower nothing will run. A feature
is one complete thing a user can do, never a layer of the app. It phases them foundations → core → dependent → polish, and **features are the unit of
parallelism**: siblings run side by side on their own branches, and the only thing that stops them is an
edge someone wrote because one genuinely needs the other.
"""
    }

    planAgents.planFeature -[plans]-> tower.featA "the feature gate — the gate, and its branch" {
      title "The one row that carries feature/add-record"
      notes """
Per feature it writes three rows and no merge ticket — there is none at this tier either. This gate names one
thing, its own branch; where its pull request goes is never written, because the loop takes this id's uuid,
names the epic gate of that tower, and reads `epic/record-box` off it. The two tickets below it carry no
metadata at all.
"""
    }

    planAgents.planFeature -[plans]-> tower.featA.featureTestA "the feature's suite — the composition tier" {
      title "The feature's suite, written before any of its tasks exist"
    }

    planAgents.planFeature -[plans]-> tower.featA.featureReviewA "the feature review ticket — the three questions" {
      title "The feature's review, over its own branch"
    }

    human -> planAgents.planTask "break this one feature into tasks" {
      title "One feature in, its chain of tasks out"
      notes """
Never asks anything. With no feature ticket in the prompt it stops with `ERROR reason=no-parent-feature`; a
feature gate carrying no `branch` stops with `ERROR reason=metadata-missing` and creates nothing — it
names no branch of its own and guesses none. Every row it does create is made with an explicit `--id` under the
gate and carries no metadata at all, so its git world is its id and nothing else. Every back-end unit becomes
two rows — the implementation,
then the micro test that follows it — and the interface stands alone, because the micro tier never tests a
component. Every row gets two edges: onto the feature's gate, and onto the one row before it.
"""
    }

    planAgents.planTask -[plans]-> tower.featA.backendA "the back-end ticket — the logic" {
      title "The head of the chain, and the only row claimable"
    }

    planAgents.planTask -[plans]-> tower.featA.unitA "the unit tests ticket — its tests, every case" {
      title "The micro tier follows the back-end row it tests, never a component"
    }

    planAgents.planTask -[plans]-> tower.featA.frontendA "the front-end ticket — the interface" {
      title "Built last, against functions already proved"
    }

    // ═ ACT II — the graph they left behind: the edges that run between two rows, the agent
    //   that wrote each, and the edge every single row has onto its own gate. That last set is
    //   the tier: nothing else in the database records which gate a row belongs to.

    tower.featA.backendA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.unitA "the logic, then its tests" {
      title "Written by plan-task — no test runs against a function nobody has written"
    }

    tower.featA.unitA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.frontendA "the tests, then the interface" {
      title "Written by plan-task — the interface is built against functions already proved"
    }

    tower.featA.frontendA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.featureTestA "the last task releases the suite" {
      title "Written by plan-task — nothing is proved against half a feature"
    }

    tower.featA.featureTestA -[blocks]-> tower.featA.featureReviewA "the suite, then the review" {
      title "Written by plan-feature — no review before the suite"
    }

    tower.featA -[blocks]-> tower.epic.epicE2e "no journey over an unmerged flow" {
      title "Written by plan-feature — the one edge only it can write"
      notes """
This is what holds the epic tier off the frontier until every feature is proved: the journeys wait on the gate,
not on any of the rows.

The edges that make a tier are not drawn, and cannot be. Every row of a feature blocks that feature's gate, and
every row of the epic tier blocks the epic gate — one edge per row, no exceptions, written by the plan agent that
created the row. But a gate is drawn as the box around its own tier, and the engine refuses an edge from a node
into the box holding it. Read them off the box: the gate's own panel is its list of blockers. A row missing its
edge still runs, and the lead agent at the gate never counts it — the gate closes over it.
"""
    }

    tower.epic.epicE2e -[blocks]-> tower.epic.epicReview "the journeys, then the review" {
      title "Written by plan-epic — no review before the journeys"
    }

    // ═ ACT III — the tower is standing, and only one row can be claimed

    human -> beads "bd list — all nine are in the tower" {
      title "Nine tickets in the database, and only one can be claimed"
      notes """
Three from the epic plan, three from the feature plan, three from the task plan — two gates and seven tickets.
The head of the chain, the back-end ticket, is ready; everything else sits behind one of the edges above, or
behind a gate, and a gate never appears in `bd ready` at all. With both tier flags off — the default — the two
gates wait for a person. The database is all the loop ever reads.
"""
      navigateTo theLoop
    }

    autoLayout LeftRight
  }

  // ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // 2 · INSTALLING THE CLI AND RUNNING THE LOOP
  // ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  dynamic view theLoop {
    title "Spider Factory/Walkthrough/2 · The run — the CLI installed, then ticket after ticket"

    include
      human,
      machine,
      machine.sfCli,
      machine.installer,
      machine.websCloner,
      machine.knobs,
      machine.preflight,
      machine.dispatcher,
      machine.agentMap,
      machine.spawnEnv,
      machine.claim,
      beads,
      tower.*,
      toolbelt,
      toolbelt.worktree,
      toolbelt.gitCli,
      toolbelt.forgeCli,
      toolbelt.claudeCli,
      forge,
      workers,
      workers.engineer,
      workers.testerUnit,
      workers.frontend,
      workers.testerFeature,
      workers.testerE2e,
      workers.reviewAgent,
      workers.leadAgent,
      stopHook,
      waiting,
      waiting.towerStalled

    // ═ ACT I — setup, once per machine: the installer, the command, the webs, the settings, the checks

    human -> machine.installer "bash CLI/install-cli.sh · put sf on your path" {
      title "You run the installer yourself — once per machine"
      notes """
No agent and no skill stands between you and the command: one bash script, run by hand, and the two things
it writes are on the node. If the parameters file does not exist yet it writes it from the shipped example;
if it already exists, your edits survive the run. If `~/.local/bin` is not on your path it prints the profile
line you are missing instead of installing something you cannot reach. Re-running it is how you repoint the
launcher after moving the repo; `CLI/uninstall-cli.sh` takes that launcher off again and touches nothing else.
"""
    }

    human -> machine.sfCli "sf · or one of its seven commands" {
      title "Everything the machine can do goes through one command"
      notes """
Seven commands, each one an engine file beside it — the seven are listed on the node itself. Bare `sf` opens a
session at the factory root and is the only arm that answers from anywhere: every other one first refuses
unless you are standing in the factory or anything nested under it, and names the root it wants. A word that is
not one of the seven is refused by name rather than guessed at. Before any engine runs it checks that the engine
file is there; for `loop` it also checks this folder answers a ticket list.
"""
    }

    human -> machine.websCloner "npm run webs:clone · the repos the workers build" {
      title "The webs themselves, once per machine, before any tower means anything"
      notes """
An npm script, not a `sf` command — the CLI has no webs command at all. Every url under `repos:` in
`webs/webs.yml` is cloned into `webs/`; a first run writes that list from the shipped example and tells you to
fill it in rather than cloning nothing quietly. A repo already sitting there is left exactly as it is.
"""
    }

    machine.knobs -> machine.dispatcher "claude · 4 parallel · 3 s · logs under ~/.spider-factory · labels *" {
      title "The settings are read once, at startup"
      notes """
At startup the loop loads its one settings file and never reads it again: the command that runs an agent, how
many workers at once, how many seconds between ticks, where the logs go, which labels it may dispatch.
Whatever you exported yourself already wins over the file.
If a number is typed wrong, the run stops there — it never quietly substitutes one for you.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> machine.preflight "before the first tick: seven checks" {
      title "Seven checks, and six of them stop the run"
      notes """
Before the first tick, if any of these is untrue the run refuses to start:
- the tools it needs are on the path
- the settings are numbers, and the tick spacing divides a minute
- both gate switches are exactly `0` or `1` — a half-written switch is not guessed at
- this folder answers a ticket list
- it can create a log folder
If only the label list is wrong, the run starts anyway and dispatches nobody — the one that bites.
"""
      navigateTo everyFailure
    }

    // ═ ACT II — one ticket, end to end

    machine.dispatcher -> beads "what is ready?" {
      title "The loop asks the database what is ready"
      notes """
The loop remembers nothing between ticks: the ticket graph is the whole state of the project.
If you restart it, it picks up in exactly the same place.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> machine.claim "round 1 — take one ticket" {
      title "Count what is already running, then take one ticket"
      notes """
If four workers are already running, this tick claims nothing.
Otherwise the database hands over one ticket, and two fields on it decide everything downstream: its `id` and
its `labels`. Nothing on the claim's metadata is read at all. A gate never appears here; gates are reached
another way, later.
"""
    }

    machine.claim -> tower.featA.backendA "the back-end ticket" {
      title "The ticket says who does it and where it lands"
      notes """
- label `backend-logic` → the engineer
- id `box-task-7f3a9c-1.3` — the uuid `7f3a9c` binds it to one tower; kind word swapped to `feature` and the
  hierarchy cut at the last dot → the feature gate `box-feature-7f3a9c-1`, whose `branch` `feature/add-record`
  is the folder it works in
- the same id, kind word swapped to `epic` and hierarchy `0` → the epic gate `box-epic-7f3a9c-0`, whose
  `branch` `epic/record-box` is what its pull request aims at
The ticket itself carries no metadata; the loop does this arithmetic and reads the two branches off the gates.
Nothing else was unblocked, which is the only reason this ticket came up.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> tower.epic "the epic gate is the ticket's own id with its kind word swapped" {
      title "The epic gate is the ticket's own id, re-kinded — never walked to, never searched for"
      notes """
Before any ticket works, the epic branch and its pull request have to exist. There is no parent chain to walk
and no listing to search: take `box-task-7f3a9c-1.3`, swap its kind word to `epic`, set its hierarchy to `0`,
and you hold `box-epic-7f3a9c-0` — the loop checks that is a real gate of type `gate`. That gate is where the
epic's branch and its pull request url live.
If the id does not read `<repo>-<kind>-<uuid>-<hierarchy>`, if a task's hierarchy carries no gate number, or if
the epic gate it names is not a gate, the ticket is refused on the spot —
`ERROR reason=no-epic-gate ticket=<id>` — and left claimed, so its lease parks it out of the queue. If the gate
it names carries no `branch`, that is `reason=gate-unworkable`, and it is left claimed the same way: no target,
no worker.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> toolbelt.forgeCli "publish the epic branch and its pull request, once" {
      title "If the epic has no pull request yet, make one before anyone works"
      notes """
If the epic gate carries no pull request url, then: give `epic/record-box` a first commit of its own off `main`,
push it, open its pull request, write the url on the gate.
Then every branch below has somewhere to point.
If any of that fails, nothing has anywhere to land — the claim goes back and the run writes why, and the next tick
tries again.
"""
    }

    toolbelt.forgeCli -> forge "PR 1 — epic/record-box into main" {
      title "Pull request 1: the epic branch into main"
      notes """
The loop is the only thing that ever publishes this branch. It also settles whether the forge is GitHub or
GitLab, and if the evidence is unclear it stops rather than guesses.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> toolbelt.worktree "make the branch's folder" {
      title "The branch gets its own folder"
      notes """
If this branch has no folder yet, make one under `.claude/worktrees/`.
If it already has one, reuse it — every later ticket on this branch works in the same folder.
If the branch is checked out somewhere else, reuse that folder and never add a second.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> machine.agentMap "backend-logic means engineer" {
      title "One label picks one worker"
      notes """
Look the ticket's one dispatching label up in the map, and that is the worker — nothing else can name a
specialist.
If the ticket has no label, or two, nobody is dispatched and the ticket stays claimed.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> toolbelt.claudeCli "start the worker in that folder" {
      title "A worker session is started in the background"
      notes """
Start a background session in that folder, then read its session id out of that worker's own spawn file — so
you can still attach to it, read it, or stop it later.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> machine.spawnEnv "pass it which ticket this is" {
      title "Hand the worker its settings before it starts"
      notes """
Eight settings go in with the session: which ticket, which branch, which target, which worker and label, where
the tower is, where the run's log is, where the done marker goes. The hook that finishes the work does not read
them from here — it rebuilds every one from this session's claims line, because the background session is pooled
and the daemon froze its environment at the run's first spawn. The folder it stands in is the worktree and is
real, but a folder belongs to a branch and not to a ticket, so it names no worker. What the hook cannot rebuild it
refuses, and the ticket stays claimed.
The eight are listed on the node itself.
"""
    }

    toolbelt.claudeCli -> workers.engineer "the rules arrive with the brief" {
      title "The worker's prompt is two rules and an order to read its ticket"
      notes """
The whole prompt is short: the ticket id, an order to read that ticket back in full as its complete brief, a
paragraph saying this folder is the worktree of a branch every task of the feature shares, and then exactly two
numbered rules — keep the lease alive with a heartbeat, and never close your own ticket, because the hook below
closes it once you say you are done.
Everything else the worker must obey — the branch rule, committing as it goes, saying what is left, starting no
other workers, and the done marker itself — is nowhere in this prompt. It lives only in the worker's own agent
file, which the session loaded when it started. What the brief sends, line by line, is on the workers layer.
The lead agent's brief is the same two rules word for word.
"""
    }

    workers.engineer -> tower.featA.backendA "write the code" {
      title "The engineer writes the code and leaves the ticket open"
      notes """
The engineer writes files into the branch's folder and lands each finished piece there with `sf commit`,
heartbeats so its claim holds, reports in its final message, and leaves the ticket open.
Closing is somebody else's job.
"""
    }

    workers.engineer -> stopHook "touch the done marker, then stop" {
      title "The worker's last act is to say it is finished"
      notes """
The loop deleted the marker before this session started, so only this session could have made it.
This agent's own `hooks:` frontmatter binds its `Stop` event to `sf hook stop`, so simply ending the session is
what runs the hook — the mechanism is on the hook node itself.
If the marker exists, the hook runs — what it does is the next three arrows.
If it does not, the hook commits nothing, pushes nothing, opens nothing and closes nothing.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> toolbelt.gitCli "commit and push, only on that marker" {
      title "Check first, then commit and push"
      notes """
If any of these is untrue, the hook refuses and says which:
- the ticket is still in progress
- the branch and the pull request target arrived with the session
- the folder is really on the branch it thinks it is
Then `sf commit` files the ticket's title as the commit — usually there is nothing left to stage, the worker
committed as it went — and the hook pushes the branch itself.
A test or a review ticket may land no files at all, and an empty tree is allowed.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> toolbelt.forgeCli "the pull request for this branch" {
      title "One branch, one pull request, forever"
      notes """
If this branch already has an open pull request, use it.
If not, make one — and the next ticket on this branch reuses it, because the branch is the unit of work, not the
ticket.
"""
    }

    toolbelt.forgeCli -> forge "PR 2 — feature/add-record into epic/record-box" {
      title "Pull request 2: the feature branch into the epic branch"
      notes """
It aims at the epic's branch, not at main. This tower has two pull requests in all: one for the epic, one for
the feature.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> beads "write the url, then close" {
      title "Write the url first, close second"
      notes """
If the url will not write onto the ticket, stop — do not close it, or the work is out there with no way back to
it.
Otherwise write the url, then close. That close is what lets the next ticket in the chain be claimed.
"""
      navigateTo everyFailure
    }

    // ═ ACT III — the same loop, six more tickets, three steps each

    machine.dispatcher -> machine.claim "the rounds — six more tickets" {
      title "What the first loop showed, six more times, said three ways"
      notes """
Everything you just watched still happens on every round, and is not said again below: the label picks the
worker, the branch gets its folder, the pull request is found or made, the worker touches the done marker, the
hook commits, pushes, writes the url and closes. Neither gate comes up in this list — `bd ready` never shows a
gate, and with both tier switches off (the default) a person closes them. Below is only the bare three: which
ticket comes up next, who its label belongs to, what that worker does.
The order never changes — logic, its tests, the interface, the suite, the review — because the loop remembers
nothing between ticks and each ticket comes up for one reason: the one before it closed.
"""
    }

    // ── the unit tests ticket — the tests for the back-end logic

    machine.claim -> tower.featA.unitA "then we pick up the unit tests ticket" {
      title "The tests for the logic just written"
      notes """
the unit tests ticket · label `unit-tests` · same branch and pull request as the back-end ticket.
"""
    }

    tower.featA.unitA -> workers.testerUnit "that ticket is handled by the unit tester" {
      title "One label, one worker"
      notes """
Nothing else could have chosen it.
"""
    }

    workers.testerUnit -> tower.featA.unitA "the unit tester tests validateRecord alone" {
      title "Every branch, every boundary, every error"
      notes """
One function, its own test file, no component in sight. Closing it opens the front-end ticket.
"""
    }

    // ── the front-end ticket — the feature's interface

    machine.claim -> tower.featA.frontendA "then we pick up the front-end ticket" {
      title "The last ticket in the feature's chain"
      notes """
the front-end ticket · label `frontend-ui` · third commit on `feature/add-record`.
"""
    }

    tower.featA.frontendA -> workers.frontend "that ticket is handled by the front-end worker" {
      title "One label, one worker"
      notes """
The interface is built against functions the micro tier has already proved.
"""
    }

    workers.frontend -> tower.featA.frontendA "the front-end worker builds the add-a-record form" {
      title "One ticket, styling included"
      notes """
Closing it opens the feature's suite.
"""
    }

    // ── the feature's suite — is the feature wired together?

    machine.claim -> tower.featA.featureTestA "then we pick up the feature's suite" {
      title "Nothing is proved against half a feature"
      notes """
the feature's suite · label `feature-test` · it waited on the front-end ticket, the last task.
"""
    }

    tower.featA.featureTestA -> workers.testerFeature "that ticket is handled by the feature tester" {
      title "One label, one worker"
      notes """
How wide each test tier looks is on the node; the label is what chose this one.
"""
    }

    workers.testerFeature -> tower.featA.featureTestA "the feature tester drives the add flow in a browser" {
      title "One case per path, nothing repeated"
      notes """
Every error and every success the feature has really reaching the screen.
Closing it opens the feature review ticket.
"""
    }

    // ── the feature review ticket — review the feature

    machine.claim -> tower.featA.featureReviewA "then we pick up the feature's review ticket" {
      title "The review of the finished branch"
      notes """
the feature review ticket · label `feature-review` · branch `feature/add-record`.
"""
    }

    tower.featA.featureReviewA -> workers.reviewAgent "that ticket is handled by the reviewer" {
      title "One label, one worker"
      notes """
The three questions it answers are on the node.
"""
    }

    workers.reviewAgent -> tower.featA.featureReviewA "the reviewer reviews the branch, findings on PR 2" {
      title "What it writes is what you read before closing"
      notes """
Comments on the pull request — no tickets filed, nothing committed.
Closing it is the last close under this gate, and that is what makes the gate ready: you close it, or with its
switch on, the lead agent does.
"""
      navigateTo theLogs
    }

    workers.reviewAgent -> workers.engineer "each finding goes once to the agent that owns that kind of fix" {
      title "One round of fixers, then review stops looking"
      notes """
One spawn per finding, one batch, one round only, routed by what the fix has to touch. The fixers commit their
own fixes with `sf commit`; review still commits nothing, re-runs nothing, and spawns no second batch. The four
constraints every prompt ends with — the last being touch no done marker, because `$SF_DONE_FILE` is review's
own and a fixer that touches it closes the review ticket mid-round — are on the node.
"""
    }

    // ═ ACT IV — the tower stops, and with both switches off only a person can start it again

    machine.dispatcher -> waiting.towerStalled "nothing left to claim" {
      title "The work is done and the loop does not know it"
      notes """
The feature is finished, but its gate is still open, and an open gate holds everything above it.
So the claim came back empty, the loop checked whether work exists anyway, found some, and wrote a waiting line
instead of an error — once a minute, until a person arrives. With this tier's switch on, the loop gets there
first: see ACT V.
"""
      navigateTo everyFailure
    }

    human -> tower.featA "read PR 2, close the feature gate" {
      title "You read pull request 2 and close the gate"
      notes """
Read pull request 2, and if the branch is right, close the feature gate.
Closing this gate IS the feature landing in the epic branch — that is what the merge is, and there is no ticket
for it anywhere in the tower.
"""
    }

    // ═ ACT V — the same gate with its switch on: the loop sends its own worker

    machine.knobs -> machine.dispatcher "SF_AGENT_FEATURE_GATES=1" {
      title "One switch hands one tier to the lead agent"
      notes """
The switch is read once, at startup, and must be exactly `0` or `1` — anything else and the run refuses to
start. It opens one tier and nothing else: `SF_AGENT_FEATURE_GATES` is the feature gates,
`SF_AGENT_EPIC_GATES` the epic's, and both default to `0`.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> beads "which gates are ready?" {
      title "A gate is listed, never claimed off the frontier"
      notes """
`bd ready` never shows a gate, so the loop asks a different question: list the gates whose blockers are all
closed. The listing returns ids and nothing else; the tier is the kind word of each id, and a tier that is not
switched on is left alone.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> workers.leadAgent "the gate dispatch spawns the lead agent" {
      title "The one worker no label reaches"
      notes """
No label is consulted — this ticket has none — and the map is not involved. Which tier the gate is comes from
the kind word of its own id, `epic` or `feature` — nothing is read from the database to work it out — and its
branch and pull-request
target come from that same id, same arithmetic as any ticket; a gate whose id reaches no usable branch writes a
waiting line, `gate_unworkable gate=<id> step=<why>`, rather than guessing. It then moves the
gate to `in_progress` itself, guarded so a second loop cannot take it too, and starts the session with
`agent=lead`, `label=gate:feature`.
"""
    }

    workers.leadAgent -> tower.featA "the lead agent proves the tier" {
      title "A closed ticket is a claim, not a fact"
      notes """
The tier is the set of tickets that block this gate, read back off the graph. Four checks, each with its number
said out loud: every one of them closed, the review actually ran and its findings answered on the branch, the
project's own test command green with its count read from the output, the pull request's pipeline green.
Any one missing: no done marker, the gate stays claimed, and a human is told which one.
"""
    }

    workers.leadAgent -> toolbelt.forgeCli "merge it, through the forge" {
      title "`gh pr merge` / `glab mr merge`, into the gate's prTarget"
      notes """
The target is the gate's **`prTarget`** — `epic/record-box` here, read off the epic gate its own id names — and
the forge does the merge. A local
`git merge origin/<prTarget>` happens only to bring the target into *this* branch when it moved underneath, and
that resolution is committed with `sf commit` so the pull request carries it.
"""
    }

    toolbelt.forgeCli -> forge "PR 2 merged into epic/record-box" {
      title "The feature is now in the epic branch"
      notes """
The lead agent reads the pull request back and confirms it says merged. It closes nothing itself.
"""
    }

    workers.leadAgent -> stopHook "touch the done marker, then stop" {
      title "The hook closes the gate, and opens no pull request"
      notes """
The claim said `kind=gate`, so the hook takes its other road: commit and push, find this branch's pull request
**at any state** (the lead agent has just merged it), write the url on the gate, close the gate, log
`closes url=… gate=true`. If the branch has no pull request at all it refuses instead — there was nothing to
merge.
"""
    }

    // ── the end-to-end ticket — the whole flow in a real browser

    machine.claim -> tower.epic.epicE2e "then we pick up the end-to-end ticket" {
      title "The broad tier's single ticket"
      notes """
the end-to-end ticket · label `epic-e2e-test` · it waited on every feature **gate**, so by the time it runs each
flow has already been merged into `epic/record-box`.
"""
    }

    tower.epic.epicE2e -> workers.testerE2e "that ticket is handled by the end-to-end tester" {
      title "The widest tier"
      notes """
One journey per thing a user can actually do, nothing repeated from the tiers below.
"""
    }

    workers.testerE2e -> tower.epic.epicE2e "the end-to-end tester walks the whole flow in a browser" {
      title "Write a record, then find it in the box"
      notes """
Real browser, real stack, on the assembled branch. Closing it opens the epic review ticket.
"""
    }

    // ── the epic review ticket — review the epic

    machine.claim -> tower.epic.epicReview "then we pick up the epic's review ticket" {
      title "The last ticket the loop ever claims"
      notes """
the epic review ticket · label `epic-review` · branch `epic/record-box`.
"""
    }

    tower.epic.epicReview -> workers.reviewAgent "that ticket is handled by the reviewer" {
      title "The same worker as the feature review"
      notes """
Nothing derives a worker from a name: the map on the node binds both labels to this one agent.
"""
    }

    workers.reviewAgent -> tower.epic.epicReview "the reviewer reviews the epic, findings on PR 1" {
      title "The whole epic at once, findings on the epic's pull request"
      notes """
After this there is nothing left to dispatch.
"""
    }

    human -> tower.epic "close the epic gate" {
      title "You close the epic gate, and the tower is finished"
      notes """
If the epic review is done, close the epic gate, and the tower is finished. With `SF_AGENT_EPIC_GATES=1` the
loop gets there first, the same way ACT V showed for the feature gate — the lead agent proves the whole tier,
merges `epic/record-box` into `main` through the forge, and its Stop hook closes the gate.

| Who | What they did |
|---|---|
| the loop | published the epic branch and its pull request, made 2 folders (one per branch), started 7 workers, closed 7 tickets |
| you | planned all 9 tickets, read the 2 pull requests, closed the 2 gates |

Both gates being yours is the default: with `SF_AGENT_FEATURE_GATES` and `SF_AGENT_EPIC_GATES` both `0`, nothing
ever dispatches a gate. Turn one on and its row moves — the loop started 9 workers and closed 9 tickets, and you
closed nothing.
"""
      navigateTo theLogs
    }

    autoLayout LeftRight
  }

  // ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // 3 · THE RUN LOG
  // ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  dynamic view theLogs {
    title "Spider Factory/Walkthrough/3 · The run log — who writes what, and how to read a tower that is not moving"

    include human, machine.dispatcher, beads, workers.engineer, stopHook, overseerAgent, logRun, logRun.*

    // ═ ACT I — one directory per run, and one line format that never varies

    machine.dispatcher -> logRun "one directory per run, made before the first tick" {
      title "One folder per run, with every file made up front"
      notes """
The files are created empty before anything happens, so a missing file and an empty file look alike and
neither can hide a silent failure — with one exception, `failures`, which is seeded like the rest and has no
writer at all. The folder is named for when the run started, and every line of every file
has one shape, defined in one file the CLI owns alone, and **exactly two callers source it**: the loop, which
holds its nine fields in process memory for the whole run, and the Stop hook, which fills the same nine from
its own environment on every line. Nothing else sources it, and there is no logging command to go through —
it was deleted, and the hook writes its lines itself. Where the hook's nine come from — this session's claims
line, rebuilt before its first line — is on the log folder itself.
"""
      navigateTo theLoop
    }

    // ═ ACT II — the writers, one file each

    machine.dispatcher -> logRun.fClaims "CLAIMS" {
      title "Every ticket taken, every gate taken, and the epic branch published"
      notes """
A dispatched line is the run's heartbeat, and a dispatch or a publish is the only thing this file ever holds —
a claim the loop puts back writes **nothing here**, so a ticket claimed and handed back leaves no trace. A ticket
you expected that never appears here was never unblocked — go look at its blockers.
A gate dispatch reads `kind=gate gate=<id> tier=<tier>` instead of a ticket id, and the epic branch's first
publish is a claims line too — `gate=<id> reason=epic-published` with the branch and the url it just wrote onto
the gate.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> logRun.fWaiting "WAITING" {
      title "The quiet ticks: alive, with nothing to do"
      notes """
Written only when a tick dispatched nothing, and at most once a minute per reason. Five reasons: two just mean
full, two mean nothing to do or something is holding it, and one is a **gate** the loop refuses to work — its id
reaches no branch or no target, which is a person's to fix, and the gate sits `open` until someone does. A gate whose tier switch is off is not a waiting line at all: it is skipped
in silence.
"""
      navigateTo everyFailure
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> logRun.fErrors "ERRORS" {
      title "Every alarm the loop saw, never capped"
      notes """
One line per alarm, naming what failed. If the tower is stuck and this file is empty, nothing is failing — read
the waiting lines instead.
"""
      navigateTo everyFailure
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> logRun.fBdStderr "bd-stderr" {
      title "The ticket tool's own complaint, kept separate"
      notes """
Whatever the claim command printed on its way out. When a claim fails, the alarm quotes this file's last line.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> logRun.fClaudeStderr "claude-stderr" {
      title "What the session counter said when it failed"
      notes """
The counter is how the loop knows it is full. It fails quietly, so without this file a broken counter looks
exactly like a full machine — forever.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> logRun.fSpawn "spawn-<ticket>" {
      title "One file per worker, holding its session id"
      notes """
The loop reads that id back out to name the worker on the log line and to close the ticket as the right actor.
One file per ticket instead of one shared file, so it is still findable hours later.
"""
    }

    workers.engineer -> logRun.fDone "done-<ticket>" {
      title "The worker's own word that it finished"
      notes """
Made by the worker as its very last action, and deleted by the loop before the worker starts. The hook commits
nothing without it.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> logRun "one tick, the four files it touches" {
      title "One ticket's whole life is four files"
      notes """
The spawn file appears when the worker starts, the done file when it declares itself finished, then a claims
line and a closes line. Search the ticket id across the folder and the whole life reads in one screen.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> logRun.fCloses "CLOSES" {
      title "Proof of work, written last"
      notes """
One line per ticket that really landed, with its pull request url, written after the commit, the push, the pull
request, and the close. So a line here is a receipt, not an intention.
A gate's close carries `gate=true` beside the url — the only way this file tells you a **gate** closed rather
than a task, and the receipt that the lead agent's merge landed.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> logRun.fResults "RESULTS" {
      title "The tally, written once at the end"
      notes """
Written when the run hits its tick limit or you press Ctrl-C, and the line says which of those ended it. How
many tickets it claimed is the number that says whether the run did anything at all.
"""
    }

    // ═ ACT III — reading a tower that is not moving

    human -> logRun "the four files, in this order" {
      title "How to read a tower that is not moving"
      notes """
| Ask | Read |
|---|---|
| Is the loop alive? | the newest folder; `results` only exists if the run ended |
| Is it failing? | `errors` — empty means nothing is failing |
| Why nothing claimed? | `waiting` — the reason, and how many workers are busy |
| Did this ticket ship? | `claims`, then `closes`, then its `spawn-<ticket>` |
| Why did this worker die? | **nothing records it** — no hook fires when a session dies, so there is no line anywhere. The tell is the absence: a `claims` line, no `closes` line, no `done-<ticket>` marker, and a ticket still `in_progress` that no `bd ready` will ever name. `failures` is created and never written, so an empty one proves nothing. Ask the overseer; it runs that check and hands you `bd update <ticket> --status failed-agent` to run yourself |
| Why won't my gate dispatch? | nothing at all is the tier's own switch being off; otherwise `waiting` for `gate_unworkable gate=<id> step=<why>`, then `errors` for `gate-claim-lost` and `worktree-failed step=gate` — all three name the gate, not a ticket |
| Why is a ticket claimed and never worked? | `errors` for `no-epic-gate` or `gate-unworkable` — both print `ticket=<id>`, and both leave it claimed on purpose |
"""
      navigateTo everyFailure
    }

    human -> overseerAgent "how's the tower going?" {
      title "Or ask the reader instead of reading the files yourself"
      notes """
`sf overseer` runs it in the foreground, over the tower in the folder you are standing in, and anything you
type after the command goes to it as the prompt. It reads those same log files, the database and git, and
answers the three questions above: is the loop alive, how far along is the tower, what is wrong.
"""
    }

    overseerAgent -> beads "reads the tower, never writes it" {
      title "Where the fix is a mutation, it hands you the command"
      notes """
`sf` is denied to it outright and its own file carries no Write, Edit or Agent tools — so it names the fault
and the command that fixes it, and you are the one who runs it. A reader that could mutate would be a second
loop nobody dispatched.
"""
    }

    beads -> logRun "and the log is not the state" {
      title "The database is the truth; the log is the testimony"
      notes """
Nothing decides anything from a log line — delete the folder and no tick changes. So a log can be rotated or
thrown away safely; what it can never be is contradictory, because it is written from the same facts the tick
acted on.
"""
      navigateTo index
    }

    autoLayout LeftRight
  }

  // ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // 4 · EVERY FAILURE
  // ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  dynamic view everyFailure {
    title "Spider Factory/Walkthrough/4 · Every failure — the same tower with every alarm firing"

    include
      human,
      machine.dispatcher,
      beads,
      stopHook,
      logRun.fErrors,
      alarms,
      alarms.startupAlarms,
      alarms.startupAlarms.*,
      alarms.tickAlarms,
      alarms.tickAlarms.*,
      alarms.stopAlarms,
      alarms.stopAlarms.*,
      waiting,
      waiting.*

    // ═ PART A — the run never starts

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.startupAlarms.toolsMissing "bd jq git + the agent command — <tool> not found · exit 1" {
      title "A tool is missing, and the run will not start"
      notes """
It checks the tools before doing anything, and quits rather than start half-ready. The Stop hook checks a
shorter list of its own, because it never starts a session.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.startupAlarms.knobNotNumeric "SF_MAX_PARALLEL=four is not a number · exit 1" {
      title "A setting is not a number, and the run will not start"
      notes """
These settings decide how many unattended sessions run, so none of them is quietly replaced with a default.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.startupAlarms.gateFlagNotASwitch "SF_AGENT_FEATURE_GATES=true is not a switch · exit 1" {
      title "A gate tier is neither a person's nor a machine's, and the run will not start"
      notes """
Each tier's switch says who closes that tier's gate: `0` a person, `1` the lead agent. `true`, `yes`, `2` are not
answers, and the loop will not pick one — a value read loosely would hand a tier's merges to a machine, or
withhold them from it, quietly.
"""
      navigateTo index
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.startupAlarms.pollUnusable "SF_POLL_S=7 is unusable · exit 1" {
      title "The poll interval cannot work, and the run will not start"
      notes """
Only 1 2 3 4 5 6 10 12 15 20 30 60 are usable: the waiting lines are one a minute, and that promise only holds
if the interval divides the minute.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.startupAlarms.noBeadsDb "no ticket database found from here · exit 1" {
      title "No ticket database here, and the run will not start"
      notes """
It asks the ticket tool whether a database answers from this directory, rather than looking for a folder — the
tool searches upward, so a database nearby is not the same as one here.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.startupAlarms.logDirUnwritable "cannot write the log folder · exit 1" {
      title "The log folder cannot be written, and the run will not start"
      notes """
Fatal on purpose: a run that cannot write its logs cannot raise an alarm, and a silent dispatcher is the one
thing this design refuses to be.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.startupAlarms.labelsStarved "SF_ALLOWED_LABELS=<typo> — the run starts and does nothing" {
      title "The one startup mistake that does NOT stop the run"
      notes """
Then no ticket ever matches a label, nothing is ever dispatched, and the waiting lines keep arriving on
schedule. Check the tickets' labels against the seven the loop knows.
"""
    }

    // ═ PART B — the tick fails, and the ticket's fate is the interesting part

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.gateClaimLost "ERROR reason=gate-claim-lost gate=<id> · no lead agent spawned" {
      title "The gate dispatch comes first, and it is the only claim that can be lost"
      notes """
Before the loop claims a single ticket it dispatches any ready gate its tier switch opens. A gate never appears
in `bd ready`, so the take is a conditional write — `bd update <gate> --status in_progress --if-status open` —
and a gate that is no longer open the instant the loop reaches for it was taken by another loop or by a hand.
The write fails, the alarm fires, and no lead agent is spawned: nothing is ever started into a gate somebody
else already holds.
"""
      navigateTo index
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.bdFailure "ERROR reason=bd-failure exit=2" {
      title "The claim command itself failed"
      notes """
Either it exited badly — its last line is quoted in the alarm — or it came back with nothing usable in it. The
next tick tries again. An empty answer is not this one: that is a waiting line.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> beads "empty claim — check whether blocked work exists" {
      title "An empty claim is two opposite situations"
      notes """
Nothing left to do, or work that exists and is blocked. The loop probes the database to write the right line,
because those two must never look alike.
"""
      navigateTo index
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.labelAmbiguous "ERROR reason=label-ambiguous · LEFT CLAIMED" {
      title "The label names no worker, or two"
      notes """
The ticket is left claimed so its five-minute lease parks it off the queue, instead of the loop spinning on an
unworkable ticket every tick. Usual cause: a ticket labelled with the wrong word, which looks identical to
having none.
"""
      navigateTo index
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.noEpicGate "ERROR reason=no-epic-gate ticket=<id> · LEFT CLAIMED" {
      title "The ticket's id reaches no gate"
      notes """
The whole git world of a ticket is read off its id, and that only reaches a gate when the id is shaped like a
tower's, reading `<repo>-<kind>-<uuid>-<hierarchy>`. An id that does not read that grammar, a task whose
hierarchy carries no gate number, or the epic gate its uuid names turning out not to be a gate — one reason,
three forms, and the ticket's own id printed on every one of them so a person can find the
row. The loop never invents a gate and never goes hunting for one; a plan bug stays a plan bug where you can
see it.
"""
      navigateTo index
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.ticketGateUnworkable "ERROR reason=gate-unworkable ticket=<id> · LEFT CLAIMED" {
      title "Its gates exist, but one carries no branch"
      notes """
The id reached both gates and one of them is empty-handed: the gate the work lands on has no `branch`, so there
is no folder to work in, or the epic gate above has none, so the pull request has nothing to aim at. A branch is
written in exactly one place — on a gate — so this is a plan bug too, and the loop never guesses one to cover it.
"""
      navigateTo index
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.epicPrFailed "ERROR reason=epic-pr-failed step=push · UNCLAIMED" {
      title "The epic's branch or pull request could not be published"
      notes """
The gate was already found two lines earlier, read off the claim's own id — nothing walks up a
graph here. The `step=` names where it died after that: the epic gate's own `branch`, the forge, its command line
tool, the branch's first commit, the push, the pull request, or writing the url back on the gate. A pull request
whose head and base are the same commit is no pull request at all, so the branch is born one commit ahead of
`main`. The ticket did nothing wrong, so every one of the seven steps puts the claim back and the next tick tries
again.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.worktreeFailed "ERROR reason=worktree-failed step=… · LEFT CLAIMED" {
      title "The branch's folder could not be made"
      notes """
It heals what it can first — a stale registration is pruned, a dead build's folder is moved aside — and only
then refuses. The `step=` says which: no starting commit resolved, the stray folder would not move, or `gate` —
the same failure on a gate dispatch, where the line names the gate instead of a ticket and puts it back to `open`
rather than leaving it claimed. Same treatment as a mislabelled ticket: a worker started into a missing folder
wastes a slot on nothing.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.branchHasUnpushed "ERROR reason=branch-has-unpushed-commits · LEFT CLAIMED" {
      title "The branch holds work no remote has ever seen, so the loop will not reset it"
      notes """
Rebuilding the folder means resetting the branch, and a branch with commits origin has never seen would lose
them. It refuses and says the recovery on the line: recover them with the reflog, or push them, then run the
loop again.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.folderMovedAside "ERROR reason=worktree-folder-moved-aside · the worker still starts" {
      title "A dead build's folder was kept, not overwritten"
      notes """
The folder existed, was not a registered worktree, and held files — what a build looks like when it dies before
registering. Git refuses to build into a folder that is not empty, so it is renamed with a `.stale-` suffix and
the branch is built fresh. The line is written and the worker is spawned anyway: nothing was deleted.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.workerNotDispatched "ERROR reason=worker-not-dispatched · put it back" {
      title "The worker never started, so the ticket goes straight back"
      notes """
The start command failed and the reason is in that worker's own spawn file — this is also what an agent name
with no file behind it looks like, including a lead agent whose file is missing. Nothing was ever started to work
this ticket, so unlike every other tick failure it is returned at once, and the next tick may well succeed. On a
gate the line names the gate instead of a ticket, and what goes back is its status, to `open`.
"""
    }

    // ═ PART C — the Stop hook refuses eighteen times, in code order

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.claimMalformedHook "ERROR reason=claim-malformed" {
      title "1 of 18 · the first refusal in the file"
      notes """
This session's line in the run's `claims` file carries no ticket or no run, so the hook cannot tell what it
stands for: no ticket, no run directory, no done marker. Only a session the loop did dispatch can reach this
line — one with **no** claims line at all exits quietly one step earlier.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.noContextHook "ERROR reason=no-context worktree=<dir>" {
      title "2 of 18"
      notes """
The dispatch context file for that worktree is unreadable **and** the claims line carries no branch or no
target to fall back on — the two are each other's backup, and with neither there is nothing to commit, push
or file. The ticket stays claimed.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.missingToolHook "ERROR reason=missing-tool tool=git" {
      title "3 of 18 · the ticket stays claimed"
      notes """
Checked before anything else the hook does, but after the check that this is a dispatched worker at all — a
session the loop never started exits quietly instead of alarming about somebody else's project.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.noWorktreeHook "ERROR reason=no-worktree" {
      title "4 of 18"
      notes """
The hook was not told which folder the work is in, and it will not guess from its own location — guessing here
means committing whatever happens to be lying around.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.ticketNotInWork "ERROR reason=ticket-not-in-work state=open" {
      title "5 of 18 · and silent when the ticket is already closed"
      notes """
The ticket is not in progress. If it is closed this is a second stop in a session that already shipped, and the
hook leaves quietly — committing onto a shared branch twice is exactly what it is there to prevent.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.noBranchHook "ERROR reason=no-branch" {
      title "6 of 18"
      notes """
The branch never reached the session, so the loop never got as far as reading it off the claim.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.noPrTarget "ERROR reason=no-pr-target" {
      title "7 of 18"
      notes """
The pull request's target never reached the session. The hook will not open a pull request into a guess.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.notDeclaredDone "ERROR reason=not-declared-done" {
      title "8 of 18 · the gate of the whole design"
      notes """
The done marker is not there: the session ended without saying, in the one place both programs read, that its
work is finished. No commit, no push, no pull request, no close. The ticket stays claimed, which parks it off
the queue, and everything chained behind it waits.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.wrongBranch "ERROR reason=wrong-branch want=feature/add-record" {
      title "9 of 18"
      notes """
The folder is not on the branch it should be — the worker was told never to change branches, and this is the
last place that can refuse to file the work under the wrong ticket's name.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.noTitle "ERROR reason=ticket-has-no-title" {
      title "10 of 18"
      notes """
The commit message is the ticket's title, so a ticket with no title cannot be filed — it would be an anonymous
commit on a shared branch.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.commitFailed "ERROR reason=commit-failed" {
      title "11 of 18 · and an empty tree is NOT this"
      notes """
The commit itself failed. A review or test ticket landing nothing is normal and is skipped quietly, so this line
means a genuine failure.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.pushFailed "ERROR reason=push-failed" {
      title "12 of 18"
      notes """
The push failed and its output is quoted in the alarm. The commit stays where it is and the ticket stays
claimed — nothing is rolled back, because a local commit is evidence, not a mistake to hide.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.forgeUnknown "ERROR reason=forge-unknown remote=<url>" {
      title "13 of 18 · reached only after the commit and the push"
      notes """
It could not tell whether this is GitHub or GitLab, and it refuses to guess: a wrong guess pushes a branch to
the wrong forge.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.forgeToolMissing "ERROR reason=missing-tool tool=gh forge=github" {
      title "14 of 18 · same reason as alarm 3, different tool"
      notes """
It knows the forge now, but that forge's command line tool is not installed. The forge name on the line is how
you tell this from the up-front check.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.prNotFoundGate "ERROR reason=pr-not-found forge=github" {
      title "15 of 18 · the gate path's own refusal"
      notes """
Reached only when the claim is a gate, and this path never opens a pull request — the lead agent was sent to
**merge** one. So the hook only looks that branch's pull request up, at any state, and finds nothing: there was
nothing to merge and the lead agent declared done anyway. Not the same failure as the next one, which is the hook
failing to raise a pull request of its own.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.prNotCreated "ERROR reason=pr-not-created forge=github" {
      title "16 of 18"
      notes """
Looking for an existing pull request and making a new one both failed, or the tool printed an error quoting some
other address — only something that reads like a pull request number counts as one.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.ticketNotAnnotated "ERROR reason=ticket-not-annotated pr=<url>" {
      title "17 of 18 · the work is out there, the ticket cannot say where"
      notes """
Writing the pull request url onto the ticket failed, so the hook stops rather than close a ticket whose pull
request nobody could find again.
"""
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.closeFailed "ERROR reason=close-failed pr=<url>" {
      title "18 of 18 · everything else already succeeded"
      notes """
The commit, the push and the pull request are all out there with no record in the tower. The ticket tool will
not close a ticket unless the closer is who it says it is — the loop claimed this one, so the hook passes the
worker's real session id; when that id is stale, this is where it surfaces.
"""
    }

    // ═ PART D — not failures: the ticks that had nothing to dispatch

    machine.dispatcher -> waiting.maxParallel "WAITING … reason=max_parallel in_progress=4" {
      title "Four workers running, so this tick claims nothing"
      notes """
The count is taken fresh from the live session listing every tick — never from a counter the loop keeps — and it
counts only the sessions still reporting themselves busy, so neither a session that died quietly nor one that
finished and now sits idle can hold a slot.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> waiting.maxParallelStalled "WAITING … reason=max_parallel_stalled in_progress=0" {
      title "Full, with nothing in progress: the slots are ghosts"
      notes """
Sessions counted busy, no ticket being worked. The two counts disagreeing is the whole alarm.
"""
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> waiting.gateUnworkable "WAITING … reason=gate_unworkable gate=<id> step=<why>" {
      title "A ready gate's id reaches no branch, or no target"
      notes """
The same refusal as a ticket whose id reaches no gate: the loop never invents a branch and never guesses a
target, and `step=` names what it could not read. Quieter here, because no lead agent was ever spawned — the gate
is never claimed, so it stays open, comes up ready again next tick, and the tier behind it simply waits. An alarm
would fire every tick forever; this is a line, capped at one a minute. A gate whose tier's switch is `0` is not a
failure at all: it is skipped in silence, which is the default on both tiers.
"""
      navigateTo index
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> waiting.frontierEmpty "WAITING … reason=frontier_empty" {
      title "The calm one: finished, or never planned"
      notes """
Nothing to claim, and the probe found no blocked work either.
"""
      navigateTo index
    }

    machine.dispatcher -> waiting.towerStalled "WAITING … reason=tower_stalled waiting=2" {
      title "Work exists and something is holding it — usually an open gate"
      notes """
The number counts work tickets only, so the gate two levels above is invisible in it and is nearly always the
cause. A gate is a checkpoint, not work: while its tier's switch is `0` — the default on both tiers — nothing
dispatches it, so the loop writes this line once a minute and waits for a person.
"""
      navigateTo index
    }

    // ═ PART E — where all of it lands

    machine.dispatcher -> logRun.fErrors "ERROR … never rate-limited" {
      title "Every alarm lands in one file, whoever saw it"
      notes """
The loop and the Stop hook write it, and unlike the waiting lines it is never capped. When the tower is stuck: the
waiting lines for the reason, this file for the refusal, then whether any session is even alive.

**The one failure with no alarm at all is a session that dies.** `Stop` is the only event a worker agent binds,
and there is no `StopFailure` event to bind — proven, not assumed: put both keys in one agent's frontmatter and
`Stop` fires while the other is refused outright as an unknown event type. So a worker killed by an API error
writes nothing here, nothing to `failures`, and nothing to the ticket: it simply stops heartbeating, stays
`in_progress`, and never appears in `bd ready` again. A gate whose lead agent died reads exactly the same way.
Nothing in this repo detects it, and nothing un-parks it — the overseer's check for a worker that died quietly is
the only thing that finds one, and all it does is name the ticket and hand a person
`bd update <ticket> --status failed-agent` to run themselves.
"""
      navigateTo theLogs
    }

    human -> beads "and every one of these is a person's call" {
      title "None of these fix themselves"
      notes """
Nothing here ever puts a claim back, un-parks a ticket, or closes the gate that refused to dispatch. A gate closes
when a person closes it, or when its tier's switch hands it to the lead agent — and neither of those repairs a
refusal. The refusals are the price of letting unattended workers commit onto shared branches, and what they buy
is a tower that stops and says why.
"""
      navigateTo index
    }

    autoLayout LeftRight
  }
}
